        body: "The engineer typically arranges cooling as independent trains — each train a matched set of air handlers, pumps, and heat-rejection equipment sized to carry its share of the IT load plus the redundancy margin. Trains are cross-connected with valved headers so any train can back up any other, and the controls rotate the lead train to equalize runtime. Heat rejection is the desert-specific art: air-cooled condensers derate badly above 110 degrees, so the engineer either oversizes them for the design condition or pairs them with adiabatic pre-cooling pads that shave the entering air temperature on the worst afternoons. Every selection is checked against the manufacturer's published capacity at the actual design condition, never at standard rating conditions that assume mild weather.\n\nInside the white space, airflow management decides whether the plant's capacity actually reaches the racks. Hot-aisle or cold-aisle containment separates supply and return air so the handlers are not fighting mixing losses, and the engineer sets the supply temperature and airflow per the contained-aisle strategy rather than flooding the room. Computational fluid dynamics modeling is standard for high-density halls: it finds the hot spots the drawings cannot show and proves the redundancy scenario — one train down, hottest day — before concrete is poured.",
