        body: "The essential electrical system is engineered from the load list up: the engineer walks every clinical space with the owner and the equipment planner, lists every device that must survive an outage — procedure lights, imaging, medical gas alarms, refrigeration, selected receptacles, nurse call, fire alarm, and the HVAC serving procedure areas — and sizes the generator for that load plus motor starting. The transfer scheme is coordinated so the life-safety and critical branches transfer within the seconds the code requires, and the generator's fuel, ventilation, and heat rejection are designed for August conditions at the site, with load-bank testing specified to prove it.\n\nMedical gas design follows the healthcare facilities code from the source to the outlet. The engineer sizes manifolds or bulk systems for the facility's outlets with the code's diversity, routes copper piping with brazed joints and the required labeling, places zone valve boxes where staff can reach them in an emergency, and designs the master and area alarm panels with the pressure thresholds the code requires. Every system is cleaned, purged, tested, and certified by an independent verifier before it serves patients — the engineer writes the verification into the specifications and reviews the test reports, because this is the system where documentation is the deliverable.",
