        body: "Stray-current protection is designed into the buried systems from the first drawings. The engineer specifies dielectric isolation at utility crossings, selects non-metallic materials where the exposure is highest, designs cathodic protection for steel that must be buried near the corridor, and details the building's grounding system so it does not become the preferred path for traction currents. Coordination with the transit agency includes sharing the corrosion-control design for review — the agency monitors stray current along the corridor and will flag designs that invite trouble.\n\nThe clearance and construction coordination is procedural but binding. The engineer maps the train's dynamic envelope and the catenary clearances onto the site plan, keeps all permanent construction and all temporary construction operations (cranes, concrete pumps, scaffolding) clear of it, and prepares the construction-safety submittals the agency requires for work near the tracks. The agency's development review covers vibration, clearances, drainage impacts on the trackway, and emergency access — the engineer treats it as a parallel permit track with its own timeline, starting at schematic design so the building's massing, foundations, and construction plan all clear agency review before the city permit set is finalized.",
