        heading: "Retention Design for Cloudburst Storms",
        body: "The engineer begins with the contributing watershed: every roof, parking lot, and landscaped area tributary to the site, with runoff coefficients honest about desert soils. The hydrograph for the design storm is routed through the proposed retention system to prove the peak water surface stays below the emergency spillway with the required freeboard, and that the basin empties within the jurisdiction's drawdown limit — commonly 24 to 36 hours — so it is ready for the next cell. Multiple storms in one monsoon week are the real test, and the engineer checks the system against back-to-back events, not just the single design storm.\n\nBasin design is grading design. The engineer shapes retention basins with the side slopes, depths, and access the jurisdiction requires, keeps the basin bottom above the seasonal high groundwater the geotechnical report identifies, and details inlets with energy dissipation so the first surge does not excavate the basin it is meant to protect. Underground storage gets the same rigor: chamber layouts with inspection ports, pretreatment ahead of every inlet, and a maintenance plan the owner can actually follow. Landscaped basins double as amenities in the dry months — the engineer just makes sure the landscaping never compromises the storage volume or the drawdown.",
