        answer: "Because older adults are physiologically the most vulnerable to heat, and a cooling failure in a 115-degree summer becomes a medical emergency within hours — not a comfort complaint. Redundant or N+1 cooling means no single compressor, pump, or control failure leaves residents without air conditioning, and alarming controls catch problems before room temperatures climb. The engineer designs this redundancy as a health requirement, sized for the sustained heat of a desert summer rather than a single peak day.",
