        body: "Rainwater harvesting is engineered from the rainfall record, not from optimism. The engineer takes the site's catchment area, applies the local precipitation data with a runoff coefficient honest about the roofing material, subtracts first-flush diversion, and sizes cistern storage for the dry intervals between monsoon seasons — showing the owner exactly which demands the harvested water can carry and which need municipal or well backup. Conveyance, filtration, and disinfection are designed for the end use: irrigation needs far less treatment than toilet flushing or cooling makeup, and the engineer matches the treatment train to each.\n\nPower and lighting tell the desert story after dark. The engineer sizes the solar array to the building's passive-reduced loads, designs battery storage for the outage resilience a remote site needs, and lays out the electrical distribution for efficient, maintainable operation. Lighting design follows the dark-sky ordinances: fully shielded fixtures, warm color temperature, lighting power below the code allowances, and controls that dim pathways after closing while keeping emergency egress lit. The engineer produces the photometric plans the dark-sky review requires, proving the design adds no sky glow — because a desert visitor center that washes out the Milky Way has failed its mission.",
